foundation.question_text:
State the name given to the third variable $t$ in the equations $x = 4t$ and $y = 2t^2$, and explain its mathematical role.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.7]
\draw[->] (-5.5,0) -- (5.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.8) -- (0,4.2) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-4,-2,2,4} {\draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1) node[below,font=\scriptsize] {$\x$};
}
\foreach \y in {2,4} {\draw (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y) node[left,font=\scriptsize] {$\y$};
}
\node[below left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,0) {$0$};
\draw[thick, blue, ->, domain=-5:5, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {0.125*\x*\x});
\node[blue, right] at (1.4, 3.4) {$y = \dfrac{x^2}{8}$};
\filldraw[blue] (-4,2) circle (2.5pt) node[above left] {$t = -1$};
\filldraw[blue] (0,0) circle (2.5pt) node[above=4pt] {$t = 0$};
\filldraw[blue] (4,2) circle (2.5pt) node[above right] {$t = 1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

development.question_text:
Explain how a parametric representation allows geometric figures such as a complete circle $x^2 + y^2 = 9$ to be traced continuously, whereas in Cartesian form two separate function branches are required.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.75]
\usetikzlibrary{angles, quotes}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (X) at (3,0);
\coordinate (P) at (50:3);
\draw[->] (-4,0) -- (4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-4) -- (0,4) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-3,3} {\draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1) node[below,font=\scriptsize] {$\x$};
}
\foreach \y in {-3,3} {\draw (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y) node[left,font=\scriptsize] {$\y$};
}
\node[below left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,0) {$0$};
\draw[thick, blue, ->] (3,0) arc (0:120:3);
\draw[thick, blue] (120:3) arc (120:360:3);
\draw[thick] (O) -- (P);
\filldraw[blue] (P) circle (2pt);
\pic[draw, "$\theta$", angle radius=0.9cm, angle eccentricity=1.5] {angle = X--O--P};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
